---
/**
 * Where every district stands, and which of them a lever actually moves.
 *
 * # Why this is its own route and not a card on `/scenario`
 *
 * It was a card there, under the histogram, and the two were answering different questions in the
 * same breath. `/scenario` answers *how much*: a total, a distribution, a ranking of the districts
 * that moved furthest. This answers *which*, and on a formula that pays the larger of a district's
 * formula amount and its guarantee those are not the same question — a lever can cost the state
 * two hundred million dollars and reach fewer than half its districts, and no figure on the other
 * page says so.
 *
 * It also reads at rest, which nothing else the runner draws does. Under current law the histogram
 * is empty and the ranking is blank, because nothing has moved; the cloud is still the shape of the
 * state, with the guarantee wall in it.
 *
 * # Why the layout is not the site's card stack
 *
 * Every other page here is a column of cards, because every other page is a set of findings a
 * reader works down. This page has one finding and it is a picture: the cards are apparatus around
 * it. So the picture takes the whole stage, the controls sit in a rail beside it where a reader can
 * hold the plot and the sliders in one view, and the prose that explains the geometry goes
 * *underneath* — which is where an explanation belongs once the thing it explains is on screen.
 *
 * The rail's controls are the scenario runner's own component, unchanged. What differs is the
 * frame around them: `.reach-rail` drops the card chrome so a reader sees levers rather than a box
 * containing levers. Restyling rather than reimplementing is what keeps the two views' levers from
 * drifting apart.
 */
import HeldFixed from "../components/HeldFixed.astro";
import ScenarioControls from "../components/ScenarioControls.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SectionAnchor from "../components/SectionAnchor.astro";
import { loadFeed, qualifiedName } from "../lib/feed.ts";
import { count, millions, pct } from "../lib/format.ts";
import {
  DEFAULT_VIEW,
  DIMENSIONS,
  DIMENSION_KEYS,
  groups,
  presets,
  scopeCounties,
} from "../lib/reach.ts";
import { LEVER_BOUNDS } from "../lib/scenario.ts";
import { modelOf } from "../lib/policy.ts";
import { refreshEffect } from "../lib/refresh.ts";
import * as routes from "../lib/routes.ts";
import { yearChip } from "../lib/year.ts";

const { bundle, alphabetical } = loadFeed();
const s = bundle.statewide;

/* The same worked example the scenario page's held-fixed card uses, priced from the feed's own
   base-cost provision rather than typed. Null where the feed prices none. */
const refresh = refreshEffect(bundle.districts, bundle.drafts, modelOf(s));

const heldShare = s.districts > 0 ? s.on_guarantee / s.districts : 0;

/*
 * The named lever positions, derived from the feed here and carried to the browser as data.
 *
 * `presets` reads the priced refresh out of the drafts, which means "Fund the plan" is the bill's
 * own multiplier rather than a number typed into a script — and where the feed prices no refresh
 * the button is not rendered at all. The full bundle is passed rather than the slim panel because
 * this runs at build time and the two agree on every field `presets` touches.
 */
const named = presets(bundle);

/* The axis menus, from the one table that also formats and labels them. */
const axes = DIMENSION_KEYS.map((key) => ({ key, label: DIMENSIONS[key].label }));

/*
 * The department's own groups, derived from the feed so the picker and the cloud cannot disagree
 * about what a code is called. Code 0 is offered like any other: three districts in this panel are
 * ones the department declines to classify, and being able to see where they sit is the point.
 */
const types = groups(bundle.districts);

/*
 * The counties, derived from the feed like the typology groups beside them, so the picker and the
 * cloud cannot disagree about what a county is called or which districts are in it. Eighty-eight,
 * by name, because that is how a reader looks for one.
 *
 * Both lists are rendered here at build rather than fetched, which is what makes this control part
 * of the document: a reader with no script sees the whole selection surface and it does nothing,
 * exactly as the axis menus above it do.
 */
const counties = scopeCounties(bundle.districts);
---

<Base
  title="Reach"
  description={`Which of Ohio's ${s.districts} school districts a change in the funding formula actually reaches, and which the guarantee holds still — every district plotted against the aid its formula computes for it.`}
  image={routes.og.page("reach")}
  imageAlt="Every Ohio school district plotted against what the formula computes for it, with the guarantee wall"
  section="reach"
>
  <h1>Reach</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    Move a lever and see which of Ohio's {count(s.districts)} districts it reaches — and which it
    passes over. In the department's FY{bundle.fiscal_year} model {count(s.on_guarantee)} of
    them, {pct(heldShare, 0)}, are paid by the guarantee rather than by the formula, and a
    formula change reaches those districts last or not at all.
  </p>

  <div id="scenario-root" data-view="reach">
    {/* The year chip, rendered here because the client cannot build one. See `scenario.astro`. */}
    <template id="scenario-chip" set:html={yearChip("formula")} />

    <div class="reach">
      <div class="reach-stage">
        {/*
          What is plotted, over the plot.

          These are not levers and are deliberately not in the rail with them: choosing an axis
          changes what the picture shows, not what the formula computes, and a reader who cannot
          tell those apart will read a change of axis as a change of policy. Sitting on the plot's
          own frame is what says "this describes the drawing below".
        */}
        <form id="reach-view" class="reach-axes">
          <label class="axis-pick">
            <span>Across</span>
            <select id="rv-x">
              {axes.map((a) => <option value={a.key} selected={a.key === DEFAULT_VIEW.x}>{a.label}</option>)}
            </select>
          </label>
          <label class="axis-pick">
            <span>Up</span>
            <select id="rv-y">
              {axes.map((a) => <option value={a.key} selected={a.key === DEFAULT_VIEW.y}>{a.label}</option>)}
            </select>
          </label>
          <label class="axis-pick">
            <span>Colour</span>
            <select id="rv-shading">
              <option value="regime" selected>How it is paid</option>
              <option value="change">Gained or lost</option>
              <option value="type">District type</option>
            </select>
          </label>
          {/*
            Which group is lit, offered only when the colour is the department's grouping.

            One group at a time against the rest, rather than nine colours at once. That is the
            palette's own limit and not a shortcut — `plot/tokens.ts` carries two hues and a
            three-step ramp, and the three steps are a measured result — but it is also the better
            answer to the question actually being asked, which is *where do the poor rural
            districts sit* rather than *decode nine categories*.
          */}
          <label class="axis-pick" id="rv-group-pick" hidden>
            <span>Which type</span>
            <select id="rv-group">
              {
                types.map((t) => (
                  <option value={t.code} selected={t.code === DEFAULT_VIEW.highlight}>
                    {t.short} ({t.districts})
                  </option>
                ))
              }
            </select>
          </label>
          <label class="axis-check">
            <input type="checkbox" id="rv-trails" checked />
            <span>Trails</span>
          </label>
        </form>

        {/*
          Which districts the reader is asking about.

          Beside the axis menus rather than in the lever rail, and for the same reason they are: a
          scope changes who the picture is *about* and not what the formula computes for them. Its
          own form because the two are read differently — every control in `#reach-view` is a value
          the view carries, and one of these is an action that adds to a set.

          # Why it is a spotlight and not a filter

          The whole state stays plotted and the selection decides what is lit. `inScope` in
          `lib/reach.ts` carries the argument: the frame is measured over all 609 districts so that a
          district which did not move looks like one that did not move, and a cloud of six is not a
          cloud — `scatterSpec` refuses fewer than twelve points, and 79 of Ohio's 88 counties hold
          fewer than twelve districts. A filter that drew only the selection would draw a blank card
          for almost every county in the state.

          # Two controls, because they are two questions

          A reader browsing counties does not have one in mind and wants to see the list; a reader
          naming a district already knows which. So the counties are checkboxes in a disclosure and
          the districts are a picker that adds one at a time — the same control `/compare` uses for
          the same task, rather than 609 checkboxes and 73 KB of markup.

          The chips are the selection, and `#rv-districts` is where the named half of it actually
          lives: a hidden field rather than a store in the script, because `readView` reads the DOM
          on every tick and a set kept anywhere else would be overwritten by the controls the first
          time a reader touched a slider.
        */}
        <form id="reach-scope" class="reach-scope">
          <div class="scope-picks">
            <label class="axis-pick">
              <span>Add a district</span>
              <select id="rv-add">
                <option value="">Choose a district…</option>
                {alphabetical.map((d) => <option value={d.irn}>{qualifiedName(d)}</option>)}
              </select>
            </label>

            <details class="scope-counties" id="rv-counties">
              <summary>
                <span class="scope-hd">Counties</span>
                <span class="scope-count" id="rv-county-count">all {counties.length}</span>
              </summary>
              <div class="scope-list">
                {
                  counties.map((c) => (
                    <label class="scope-county">
                      <input type="checkbox" name="co" value={c.slug} />
                      <span>{c.name}</span>
                      <span class="n">{c.districts}</span>
                    </label>
                  ))
                }
              </div>
            </details>
          </div>

          {/*
            The named districts, as a value the DOM carries. Written by the script, never by a
            reader, and read back by `readView` — see the note above.
          */}
          <input type="hidden" id="rv-districts" value="" />

          {/*
            What is selected, and the only way to unselect it. Empty and hidden until there is a
            scope: a row reading "Every district" beside a control that already says so is a chip
            that never does anything.
          */}
          <div class="chips" id="rv-chips" aria-live="polite" hidden></div>
        </form>

        <noscript>
          <div class="card err" id="needs-script" data-part="needs-script">
            <h2><SectionAnchor id="needs-script" />This page needs JavaScript</h2>
            <p class="note">
              Every other page on this site carries its figures in the document and reads correctly
              without any script. This one re-derives Ohio's funding formula over all {
                count(s.districts)
              } districts as you move a slider, and plots the result, which is not something a
              pre-rendered page can do.
            </p>
            <p class="note">
              The underlying data is still available: <a href="/data">the feed and a CSV of the
              panel</a>, and <a href="/districts">every district's published figures</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </noscript>

        {/*
          One container, unlike `/scenario`'s three. There is no forecast here — the band is a
          different claim, gated on different checks, and it is drawn once on the page where it is
          the subject — and no detail half, because the cloud is the whole of this view.
        */}
        <div id="scenario-out"></div>
      </div>

      <aside class="reach-rail">
        <div class="presets">
          <h2 class="rail-hd">Start from</h2>
          <div class="preset-row">
            {
              named.map((p) => (
                <button
                  type="button"
                  class="preset"
                  aria-pressed={p.key === "law" ? "true" : "false"}
                  data-preset={JSON.stringify(p.levers)}
                >
                  <span class="preset-label">{p.label}</span>
                  <span class="preset-note">{p.note}</span>
                </button>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <p class="note">
            Every one of these is a position the sliders below can hold. That is a real
            constraint rather than a note about the buttons: base cost stops at {
              LEVER_BOUNDS.baseCostScale.max
            }×, and some districts do not clear their own guarantee anywhere inside that range. A
            preset for one of them would name a setting this model cannot be put in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <ScenarioControls
          model={s.minimum_state_share}
          onGuarantee={s.on_guarantee}
          projection={{ available: false, baseYear: 0, maxYear: 0 }}
          refresh={refresh}
        />
      </aside>
    </div>

    <p class="note" id="scenario-status"></p>
  </div>

  {/*
    The apparatus, under the thing it is apparatus for.

    The geometry has to be stated in prose somewhere — the diagonal is a law and not a trend line,
    and a reader who does not know that reads the picture as a correlation — but stating it above
    the picture puts three paragraphs between a reader and the only thing on the page.
  */}
  <div class="card" id="what-this-is" data-part="what-this-is">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="what-this-is" />What you are looking at</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Each district is one point. On the pair the page opens with, that is what the formula computes
      for it across the bottom and what it is actually paid up the side, both per pupil — and{" "}
      <strong>nothing can fall below the diagonal</strong>. That is not a trend in the data, it is
      the rule. A district receives the larger of its formula amount and its guarantee, so a point
      on the line is paid by the formula and a point above it is held above the formula, with the
      vertical distance being the guarantee itself.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      That is what makes the picture worth having. A district <strong>on</strong> the line travels{" "}
      <em>up</em> it when the formula gets more generous. A district <strong>above</strong> it
      travels <em>sideways</em> — its formula amount rises, its payment does not, because the
      guarantee absorbs the difference — until it reaches the line, and only then does another
      dollar of base cost reach it. <strong>A flat trail is a district your settings do not
      reach.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The other axes answer the next question, which is <em>who</em> those districts are. Valuation
      per pupil, the disadvantaged share and enrolment are properties of the district that no lever
      here moves, so against one of them a district can only travel in the aid direction — which is
      exactly what makes the pattern legible. Base cost per pupil does move with its lever; state
      share and local capacity are as the department published them.
    </p>
  </div>

  {/* The same limits as the scenario runner, because these are the same levers. */}
  <HeldFixed districts={bundle.districts} total={s.districts} refresh={refresh} />

  <div class="card" id="baseline" data-part="baseline">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="baseline" />What the levers move away from</h2>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>Current law here is <a href={routes.wikiNode("legislation", "hb-96-2025")}>Am. Sub.
      H.B. 96 as enacted</a></strong>, which pays {
        millions(s.realized_aid_total).replace("+", "")
      } across {count(s.districts)} districts in FY{bundle.fiscal_year}, {
        millions(s.guarantee_total).replace("+", "")
      } of it guarantee money. Every trail starts at that act's own position for the district, so
      with the controls untouched there is nothing to see moving — which is the correct answer, and
      the shape of the state is there either way.
    </p>
  </div>

  <script>
    import "../scripts/scenario.ts";
  </script>
</Base>
